        "description": "Map showing the territorial extent of the Ahom Kingdom (18th Century)\nDuring the 18th century, the Ahom kingdom's western frontier was defined by the Manas River on the north bank of the Brahmaputra and the Nagarberra Hills on the south bank, while its eastern reach extended to Sadiya and the Patkai Hills. The Eastern Himalayan range formed its northern boundary, and the southern frontier was anchored by Raha in Central Assam and the Naga-Mikir Hills in Upper Assam. \n\n(Note: This revised map is derived from File:Ahom-kingdom-c1826p.png and incorporates critical corrections to accurately reflect 18th-century geopolitics. The Kamrup and Darrang Duar regions have been removed because they were under Bhutanese rule. The Doboka region has been excluded as it was under Kachari rule, accurately establishing the Raha region as the southern border. The Dimapur-Upper Dhansiri region has been omitted, as it served as a forested buffer zone between the Ahom and Kachari states and was never occupied by the Ahoms.",
